import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths
import ../../../Ui/PageHero

meta = {
	title: "Crypto",
	description: "The Roc Solid server platform's crypto: secure random bytes, AES-GCM and P-256 (key pairs, ECDH, ES256) from the host, with the crypto package's SHA-256, HMAC, HKDF, Base64, Base32, Hex and SHA-1 (for authenticator apps' codes), for tokens, signed links, sealed secrets and Web Push.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "tokens", label: "Tokens, and their digests" },
		{ id: "links", label: "Signed links" },
		{ id: "sealed", label: "Sealed secrets" },
		{ id: "p256", label: "P-256: ES256 and ECDH" },
		{ id: "modules", label: "The modules" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The platform · crypto"
				title="Secrets, and what to do with them"
			>
				Three things most apps need: tokens nobody can guess (and stored so a leaked table is useless),
				links that can't be forged, and secrets stored encrypted. Secure random bytes, AES-GCM and the
				elliptic curve P-256 come from the host. The rest of the crypto is plain Roc, checked against
				published test vectors, in
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}><code>packages/crypto</code></a>. The secrets themselves come from
				the app's <a href={Paths.platform_settings}>environment</a>.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				The samples are from <code>server/test/app/Samples.roc</code>, which the platform's tests compile
				and run. It names the crypto package beside the platform in its header
				(<code>crypto: "../packages/crypto/main.roc"</code>) and imports <code>crypto.Hmac</code> and the
				rest from it.
			</p>

			<Heading id="tokens">Tokens, and their digests</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_tokens.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_tokens.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>Random.bytes!(count)</code> is the operating system's secure randomness (through
				<code>ring</code>). If the system has none to give, the call crashes rather than make a key from
				anything weaker. A sign-in link's token is 32 random bytes. The database keeps only its SHA-256
				digest, so a copy of the table signs no one in.
			</p>

			<Heading id="links">Signed links</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_links.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_links.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				The app derives a key for signing links from its <code>APP_SECRET</code> with HKDF, so that key
				serves this one purpose alone, then signs what the link says with HMAC-SHA256. To check a link,
				<code>Hmac.verify_sha256</code> compares signatures in constant time, so how long it takes doesn't
				tell a forger how much of a signature was right.
			</p>

			<Heading id="sealed">Sealed secrets</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_sealed.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_sealed.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>AesGcm.seal!(key, plaintext)</code> encrypts with AES-256-GCM under a 32-byte key
				(<code>BadKey</code> otherwise), with a fresh random 12-byte nonce each time. It returns the nonce,
				the ciphertext and the 16-byte tag, one after another: Web Crypto's output with the nonce in front,
				so either side can open what the other sealed. <code>AesGcm.open!</code> answers
				<code>Forged</code> for anything changed, or sealed under another key.
			</p>

			<Heading id="p256">P-256: ES256 and ECDH</Heading>
			<Code title={"${Sources.platform_es256.file}, in part"} source={Sources.platform_es256.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>
				<code>P256</code> is the elliptic curve P-256 (secp256r1), as much of it as
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_push}>Web Push</a> needs. A public key is an uncompressed point, 65 bytes
				(<code>4</code>, then <code>x</code> and <code>y</code>), and a private key its 32-byte scalar: the
				raw forms browsers, Web Crypto and <code>web-push generate-vapid-keys</code> use, in base64url.
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"P256.generate!({})"}</code> makes a new key pair from the operating system's random bytes:
					<code>{"{ public_key, private_key }"}</code>, 65 and 32 bytes, which <code>sign!</code> signs
					with. The push plugin makes its VAPID keys with it under <code>--dev</code>. Keep a private key in
					the server's environment, never in the app's code.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>P256.sign!(private_key, public_key, message)</code> is an ES256 signature (ECDSA on P-256
					over SHA-256): 64 bytes, <code>r</code> then <code>s</code>, as a JWT carries it. Each signature
					is different, since ECDSA draws a random number, and each verifies. It answers <code>BadKey</code>
					unless the two keys are a pair, so signing once also checks a pair from the environment.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>P256.ecdh!(peer)</code> makes a key pair for this one call, agrees a secret with
					<code>peer</code>'s public key, and forgets the private key: it answers the new public key, for the
					other side, and the 32-byte shared secret, which only the holder of <code>peer</code>'s private key
					can compute too. Derive keys from the secret with HKDF; never use it as a key. Web Push encrypts
					each message this way (RFC 8291). It answers <code>BadKey</code> unless <code>peer</code> is a point
					on the curve.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>AesGcm.seal_with_nonce!(key, nonce, plaintext)</code> is AES-GCM with a 12-byte nonce of the
					caller's, for a protocol that derives its own: AES-128 under a 16-byte key (Web Push's
					<code>aes128gcm</code>, RFC 8188), AES-256 under a 32-byte one. It answers the ciphertext, then the
					tag. A nonce used twice under one key gives the key away, so anything else should use
					<code>AesGcm.seal!</code>, which draws one.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<Heading id="modules">The modules</Heading>
			<table>
				<thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><td><code>AesGcm</code></td><td><code>seal!</code>, <code>open!</code>: AES-256-GCM, by the host; <code>seal_with_nonce!</code>: AES-128-GCM or AES-256-GCM with the caller's nonce</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>P256</code></td><td><code>generate!</code>: a new key pair; <code>sign!</code>: ES256 signatures; <code>ecdh!</code>: a shared secret with a key pair made for one call</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>Random</code></td><td><code>bytes!(count)</code>, <code>seed_u64!</code>: the operating system's randomness</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Sha256</code></td><td><code>hash</code>: 32 bytes; <code>hex</code>: 64 hex digits</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Sha1</code></td><td><code>hash</code>: 20 bytes; <code>hex</code>. For authenticator apps' codes (TOTP) alone: never sign with it or keep a digest in it</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Hmac</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, message)</code>; <code>verify_sha256(key, message, signature)</code> and <code>equal</code>, in constant time; <code>sha1</code> and <code>verify_sha1</code>, for TOTP</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Hkdf</code></td><td><code>sha256(key, salt, info, length)</code> (RFC 5869), <code>TooLong</code> past 8160 bytes; <code>extract</code>, <code>expand</code></td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Base64</code></td><td><code>encode</code>, <code>decode</code>; <code>url_encode</code> (unpadded), <code>url_decode</code>; <code>encode_unpadded</code>, <code>url_encode_padded</code>, <code>lines</code> (MIME's)</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Base32</code></td><td><code>encode</code>, <code>encode_unpadded</code> (an <code>otpauth://</code> secret), <code>decode</code> (either case)</td></tr>
					<tr><td><code>crypto.Hex</code></td><td><code>encode</code>, <code>decode</code></td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
			<p>
				<code>AesGcm</code>, <code>P256</code> and <code>Random</code> are the platform's, done by the host
				(<code>ring</code>). The rest belong to
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_crypto}>the crypto package</a>: pure Roc with no effects, so they run
				anywhere, including a page in the browser and <code>expect</code> tests. They are checked against
				the test vectors of NIST, RFC 2202, RFC 4231, RFC 4648 and RFC 5869; <code>Sha256</code> wraps
				Roc's builtin <code>Crypto.SHA256</code>. Decoding is strict: only the alphabet, no spaces,
				padding left out or exactly right, and the last character's unused bits zero (Hex and Base32 take
				either case, as a person types a secret).
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
